---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I ship chilli and paprika by sea or air?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Sea freight is the standard for bulk orders — far lower cost per kilogram but slower (weeks). Air freight is faster (days) and suits samples, small orders or urgent restocks, at a higher cost. Match the method to order size and urgency."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What documents do I need to clear chilli through customs?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The typical set is the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin, health certificate, phytosanitary certificate, certificate of analysis and specification sheet. Confirm the exact set for your destination country."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What Incoterm should I use for chilli imports?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "FOB (seller loads on board) and CIF (seller covers freight and insurance to the destination port) are the most common for chilli imports. EXW puts the most responsibility on the buyer; DAP/DDP on the seller."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sea vs Air Freight for Chilli Imports | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Sea vs air freight for chilli and paprika imports — cost, transit time, customs documents and Incoterms (FOB, CIF) explained."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-02"
  dateModified="2026-09-02"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Sea vs Air Freight
    </nav>
    <h1>Sea vs Air Freight for Chilli Imports</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Ship bulk orders by sea, small or urgent orders by air. Prepare the customs document set before
      the goods arrive, and pick an Incoterm that matches how much shipping control you want.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Sea is for bulk and cost, air is for small or urgent orders — the break-even is volume, not speed alone. Chilli is a dry, dense product that ships well by container, but the customs document set (phytosanitary, COA, invoice, packing list) must be prepared before the goods arrive.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Sea freight vs air freight</h2>
    <p>The first logistics decision comes down to cost versus speed:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Sea freight</th><th>Air freight</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Cost per kg</strong></td><td>Low — standard for bulk</td><td>High — premium for speed</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Transit time</strong></td><td>Weeks</td><td>Days</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Full-container bulk, regular supply</td><td>Samples, small orders, urgent restock</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For most food manufacturers the pattern is clear: sea freight for production supply, air freight
      for samples and urgent gaps. Dried chilli is dense and shelf-stable, so it stores well in a
      container — sea is the default for regular orders.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The customs document checklist</h2>
    <p>A complete import document set keeps clearance smooth:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Commercial invoice</strong> — value and terms of sale</li>
      <li><strong>Packing list</strong> — quantities, weights, package breakdown</li>
      <li><strong>Bill of lading</strong> — proof of shipment and carrier</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate of origin</strong> — for tariff treatment</li>
      <li><strong>Health certificate</strong> — food-safety attestation</li>
      <li><strong>Phytosanitary certificate</strong> — chilli is a plant product</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate of analysis (COA)</strong> — batch colour, heat, moisture</li>
      <li><strong>Specification sheet</strong> — agreed variety, SHU, ASTA, mesh, moisture</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Incoterms, in one line each</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>EXW</strong> — buyer arranges everything from the factory gate</li>
      <li><strong>FOB</strong> — seller loads on board; buyer arranges freight and insurance</li>
      <li><strong>CIF</strong> — seller covers freight and insurance to the destination port</li>
      <li><strong>DAP / DDP</strong> — seller delivers to destination (DDP includes duty)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      FOB and CIF are the workhorses for chilli imports. Choose FOB if you have a preferred forwarder;
      CIF if you want the seller to manage the ocean leg. See the full
      <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/shipping-and-customs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shipping &amp; customs reference</a> in the Knowledge Hub.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The break-even is volume, not speed</h2>
    <p>
      Air freight is not simply "faster sea freight" — it is a different cost curve. Air charges by
      weight and rises steeply with every kilogram; sea charges by container and flattens as you fill
      it. That is why the switch from air to sea is almost never a speed decision but a
      <em>volume</em> decision: once an order exceeds roughly a pallet or two of product, sea wins on
      cost so decisively that the extra weeks stop mattering. Most regular food-manufacturing supply
      runs on sea, with air reserved for samples, launch runs and emergency restocks.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why chilli ships well by container</h2>
    <p>
      Dried chilli and paprika are dense, low-moisture, shelf-stable products — the profile that
      travels well. The two things to manage in transit are <strong>moisture pickup</strong> and
      <strong>heat</strong> inside the container, both of which accelerate colour loss. Desiccant,
      sealed inner liners and ventilated-but-not-wet conditions keep the product stable across a
      multi-week ocean leg. For long voyages, this is where the colour-and-storage discipline from
      <a href="/blog/chilli-powder-colour-storage/">our colour guide</a> meets the shipping decision.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Lead time, planned</h2>
    <p>
      Plan the whole door-to-door chain, not just the sailing time: production and packing, export
      customs, ocean transit, then import clearance and inland delivery. Sea freight can add several
      weeks of inventory pipeline, so buyers on a just-in-time schedule either hold buffer stock or
      reserve air freight for the gaps. A forwarder can map the exact chain for your origin and
      destination — and the document checklist above is what keeps each hand-off moving.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Should I ship chilli and paprika by sea or air?</h3>
      <p>Sea freight is the standard for bulk orders — far lower cost per kilogram but slower (weeks). Air freight is faster (days) and suits samples, small orders or urgent restocks, at a higher cost. Match the method to order size and urgency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What documents do I need to clear chilli through customs?</h3>
      <p>The typical set is the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin, health certificate, phytosanitary certificate, certificate of analysis and specification sheet. Confirm the exact set for your destination country.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What Incoterm should I use for chilli imports?</h3>
      <p>FOB (seller loads on board) and CIF (seller covers freight and insurance to the destination port) are the most common for chilli imports. EXW puts the most responsibility on the buyer; DAP/DDP on the seller.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Ask About Shipping &amp; Documents</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/products/dried-chillies/" style="margin-left:8px;">Browse Dried Chillies</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
